WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.491
During exercise, muscle
cells need energy faster.

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:05.008
Breathing and heart rate usually
rise to help deliver oxygen and

00:00:05.008 --> 00:00:07.307
glucose and remove carbon dioxide.

00:00:07.803 --> 00:00:11.299
Now, what if demand rises
faster than oxygen delivery?

00:00:11.696 --> 00:00:14.067
The muscles send an angry delivery review?

00:00:14.852 --> 00:00:18.124
They can obtain extra energy
through an anaerobic pathway.

00:00:18.571 --> 00:00:20.544
Anaerobic means without oxygen.

00:00:21.201 --> 00:00:23.926
Aerobic respiration
doesn't simply switch off.

00:00:24.231 --> 00:00:26.137
The pathways can contribute together

00:00:26.932 --> 00:00:31.600
So an emergency helper joins in rather
than the entire factory changing overnight

00:00:32.263 --> 00:00:36.770
For this school topic, the human
anaerobic word equation is glucose

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.645
gives lactic acid with much less
energy released per glucose.

00:00:41.041 --> 00:00:44.950
In the body, we usually call
the substance lactate And

00:00:44.952 --> 00:00:47.188
carbon dioxide too, like before?

00:00:47.969 --> 00:00:50.980
Not in this simplified
human anaerobic equation.

00:00:51.467 --> 00:00:52.662
That's an exam trap.

00:00:53.004 --> 00:00:58.344
Keep the human pathway separate from the
yeast pathway we will meet next Glucose

00:00:58.361 --> 00:01:01.873
to lactic acid, less energy, no oxygen

00:01:01.873 --> 00:01:03.253
required by that pathway

00:01:03.784 --> 00:01:05.662
Why less energy per glucose?

00:01:06.628 --> 00:01:11.463
Because the glucose is only partly broken
down, so energy remains in the products

00:01:12.317 --> 00:01:13.100
Exactly.

00:01:13.434 --> 00:01:16.759
During intense activity,
lactate production can rise.

00:01:17.138 --> 00:01:18.771
Fatigue has several causes.

00:01:19.036 --> 00:01:21.946
Lactate is not simply a
villain that causes every ache.

00:01:22.233 --> 00:01:24.909
It isn't the explanation for
next-day muscle soreness.

00:01:25.586 --> 00:01:29.843
So blaming yesterday's stairs on a
bottle of lactic acid would be too simple

00:01:30.603 --> 00:01:31.164
Yes.

00:01:31.581 --> 00:01:35.752
After exercise, breathing and heart
rate remain elevated during recovery.

00:01:36.177 --> 00:01:39.524
Your school may call the extra
oxygen requirement oxygen debt.

00:01:40.018 --> 00:01:43.594
Recovery includes processing lactate
and restoring the body towards its

00:01:43.594 --> 00:01:50.075
resting state So I stopped running, but
the recovery team hasn't finished Good.

00:01:50.416 --> 00:01:54.196
A sprinter relies more heavily
on rapid anaerobic energy supply

00:01:54.378 --> 00:01:55.791
than a steady distance runner.

00:01:56.141 --> 00:01:58.221
Both still use aerobic respiration.

00:01:58.555 --> 00:02:01.525
You cannot maintain an
all-out sprint indefinitely.

00:02:01.859 --> 00:02:04.609
Meet yeast, a single-celled fungus.

00:02:04.957 --> 00:02:07.404
Without oxygen, it can
carry out fermentation.

00:02:07.945 --> 00:02:09.705
What do you predict its products are?

00:02:10.464 --> 00:02:11.452
Lactic acid.

00:02:11.742 --> 00:02:16.916
I finally memorized it Right
answer for humans, wrong organism.

00:02:17.137 --> 00:02:20.599
Yeast fermentation produces
ethanol and carbon dioxide.

00:02:20.947 --> 00:02:25.796
The board now reads glucose gives ethanol
plus carbon dioxide with a small amount

00:02:25.796 --> 00:02:32.692
of energy released Humans, lactic acid;
yeast, ethanol and carbon dioxide.

00:02:33.199 --> 00:02:37.232
Label the organism before
answering That could rescue a mark.

00:02:37.633 --> 00:02:39.050
Watch our dough illustration.

00:02:39.286 --> 00:02:41.662
Yeast produces carbon dioxide bubbles.

00:02:41.981 --> 00:02:43.466
What makes bread dough rise?

00:02:44.345 --> 00:02:48.194
The carbon dioxide gas, not
yeast doing tiny push-ups

00:02:48.982 --> 00:02:49.771
Exactly.

00:02:50.113 --> 00:02:53.572
Baking sets the structure, and
much of the ethanol evaporates.

00:02:53.874 --> 00:02:57.267
In brewing, ethanol is the
alcohol people want to produce.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:03.589
Same biological process, different useful
products Can we see the gas being made?

00:03:04.368 --> 00:03:08.380
A supervised demonstration can use
yeast, sugar, and warm water in

00:03:08.381 --> 00:03:09.856
a bottle with a balloon over it.

00:03:10.301 --> 00:03:12.178
Gas production can inflate the balloon.

00:03:12.553 --> 00:03:14.421
Warm, not boiling water.

00:03:14.676 --> 00:03:16.503
Excessive heat can kill yeast.

00:03:16.852 --> 00:03:20.410
Don't tightly seal a rigid container,
and don't drink the mixture

00:03:21.212 --> 00:03:24.731
And compare it with a control
rather than declare one balloon

00:03:24.731 --> 00:03:25.972
a scientific breakthrough

00:03:26.799 --> 00:03:27.494
Excellent.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:32.133
A control without live yeast helps
test whether live yeast is responsible.

00:03:32.469 --> 00:03:35.950
Keep amounts, temperature, and
observation time comparable.

00:03:36.316 --> 00:03:38.307
Three columns, one challenge.

00:03:38.672 --> 00:03:43.104
Aerobic respiration: oxygen,
energy per glucose, products.

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:43.723
Go

00:03:44.531 --> 00:03:50.346
Uses oxygen, more energy, carbon
dioxide and water Human anaerobic

00:03:50.391 --> 00:03:57.266
pathway No oxygen required, less
energy per glucose, lactic acid or

00:03:57.270 --> 00:04:03.730
lactate in the body Yeast fermentation
No oxygen required, less energy per

00:04:03.733 --> 00:04:06.535
glucose, ethanol and carbon dioxide

00:04:07.301 --> 00:04:09.541
All three release energy from glucose.

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:13.241
Don't write that anaerobic
respiration releases more energy

00:04:13.241 --> 00:04:14.987
just because sprinting feels harder.

00:04:15.629 --> 00:04:20.023
Energy per glucose and how quickly
energy is needed are different questions

00:04:20.755 --> 00:04:20.969
That

00:04:20.969 --> 00:04:21.802
is sneaky.

00:04:22.269 --> 00:04:25.802
Harder effort doesn't mean a more
efficient fuel recipe Our plant

00:04:25.813 --> 00:04:27.862
has a day shift and a night shift.

00:04:28.296 --> 00:04:30.033
Does it only respire at night?

00:04:30.744 --> 00:04:34.548
Yes, because during the day
it's busy photosynthesizing

00:04:35.319 --> 00:04:37.185
My imaginary tissues are back.

00:04:37.689 --> 00:04:39.665
Plants respire day and night.

00:04:40.098 --> 00:04:43.846
Photosynthesis and respiration are
different processes that can happen

00:04:43.846 --> 00:04:49.949
at the same time So sunlight doesn't
give mitochondria the day off Correct.

00:04:50.513 --> 00:04:54.528
Photosynthesis uses light energy
to make glucose from carbon dioxide

00:04:54.528 --> 00:04:56.397
and water, releasing oxygen.

00:04:56.868 --> 00:05:00.234
It takes place in cells with the
right photosynthetic machinery,

00:05:00.423 --> 00:05:05.887
including chloroplasts in green plant
tissues Photosynthesis stores energy.

00:05:06.323 --> 00:05:10.759
Respiration releases energy for
cell activities In sufficiently

00:05:10.765 --> 00:05:14.469
bright light, photosynthesis
can be faster than respiration.

00:05:14.961 --> 00:05:20.149
The plant's net gas exchange then takes
in carbon dioxide and releases oxygen.

00:05:20.471 --> 00:05:25.233
At night, photosynthesis stops,
but respiration continues, using

00:05:25.244 --> 00:05:27.504
oxygen and releasing carbon dioxide

00:05:28.271 --> 00:05:31.712
Net means the overall result
of both processes, not that

00:05:31.713 --> 00:05:33.505
respiration disappeared in daylight

00:05:34.323 --> 00:05:35.131
Exactly.

00:05:35.438 --> 00:05:39.188
Roots need energy too, even though
they aren't normally photosynthesizing.

00:05:39.606 --> 00:05:43.263
Remember our three labels:
cells respire, chloroplasts

00:05:43.272 --> 00:05:45.844
photosynthesize, lungs ventilate.

00:05:46.219 --> 00:05:49.372
Can you see a chemical process
just by staring at a seed?

00:05:50.256 --> 00:05:54.414
Only if I draw a cartoon
face on it We need evidence.

00:05:54.907 --> 00:05:59.103
First, carbon dioxide turns
limewater cloudy or milky.

00:05:59.638 --> 00:06:03.335
In a properly arranged school
experiment, exhaled air turns it

00:06:03.335 --> 00:06:07.610
cloudy faster than inhaled air because
it contains more carbon dioxide.

00:06:08.272 --> 00:06:12.263
So a color change is our clue,
not proof that lungs are the only

00:06:12.263 --> 00:06:14.833
place doing respiration Good.

00:06:15.341 --> 00:06:19.064
This uses laboratory chemicals and
equipment, so follow your teacher's

00:06:19.079 --> 00:06:20.877
setup and safety instructions.

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:23.964
Never suck limewater
through a tube or taste it

00:06:24.777 --> 00:06:27.211
A sensible rule even before the exam

00:06:27.895 --> 00:06:33.151
Second, compare germinating seeds with
boiled dead seeds in insulated flasks.

00:06:33.575 --> 00:06:36.396
Start at the same temperature
and use comparable amounts.

00:06:36.729 --> 00:06:40.553
The living seeds can cause a temperature
rise as respiration releases heat

00:06:41.356 --> 00:06:42.868
The dead seeds are the control.

00:06:43.345 --> 00:06:45.525
But what if microbes also produce heat?

00:06:46.315 --> 00:06:48.632
Then we could misinterpret the result.

00:06:49.179 --> 00:06:52.678
The school procedure reduces
microbial contamination and

00:06:52.678 --> 00:06:54.280
controls other conditions.

00:06:54.663 --> 00:06:58.056
We compare the change in temperature,
not just whichever thermometer

00:06:58.060 --> 00:06:59.881
happens to read higher at the start
